1
00:00:00,116 --> 00:00:03,228
Hello and welcome back to J D Science.

2
00:00:03,622 --> 00:00:07,396
In today's lesson, we are going to explore the three states

3
00:00:07,465 --> 00:00:11,355
of matter: solids, liquids and gases.

4
00:00:11,749 --> 00:00:15,406
We will examine how their particles are arranged, how

5
00:00:15,499 --> 00:00:19,180
they move, and how this explains the different properties

6
00:00:19,238 --> 00:00:20,318
of each state.

7
00:00:20,713 --> 00:00:24,207
By the end of this lesson, you should be able to describe

8
00:00:24,242 --> 00:00:27,853
the arrangement and movement of particles in solids,

9
00:00:27,911 --> 00:00:31,939
liquids and gases, explain their properties using

10
00:00:31,997 --> 00:00:35,771
the particle model, and answer G C S E

11
00:00:35,922 --> 00:00:38,616
examination questions confidently.

12
00:00:38,859 --> 00:00:41,193
Let us begin with a simple question.

13
00:00:41,588 --> 00:00:45,616
Why does a piece of ice maintain its shape, while water

14
00:00:45,686 --> 00:00:49,262
flows and steam spreads throughout its surroundings?

15
00:00:49,703 --> 00:00:53,697
The answer lies in the arrangement and movement of particles.

16
00:00:54,080 --> 00:00:57,052
Everything around us is made of particles.

17
00:00:57,447 --> 00:01:01,278
In the particle model, these particles are represented as

18
00:01:01,394 --> 00:01:02,810
small spheres.

19
00:01:03,263 --> 00:01:07,373
The way they are arranged and the energy they possess determine

20
00:01:07,408 --> 00:01:11,250
whether a substance exists as a solid, liquid or

21
00:01:11,320 --> 00:01:11,912
gas.

22
00:01:12,365 --> 00:01:14,106
Let us begin with solids.

23
00:01:14,443 --> 00:01:18,240
In a solid, particles are packed closely together, usually

24
00:01:18,356 --> 00:01:20,295
in a regular arrangement.

25
00:01:20,538 --> 00:01:24,358
Strong forces of attraction hold the particles in fixed

26
00:01:24,428 --> 00:01:25,217
positions.

27
00:01:25,461 --> 00:01:29,048
However, the particles are not completely stationary.

28
00:01:29,129 --> 00:01:31,916
They vibrate about their fixed positions.

29
00:01:32,043 --> 00:01:35,967
Because the particles cannot move freely from one position

30
00:01:36,026 --> 00:01:39,892
to another, a solid has a fixed shape and a fixed

31
00:01:39,973 --> 00:01:40,519
volume.

32
00:01:40,762 --> 00:01:44,478
Solids are also very difficult to compress because their

33
00:01:44,547 --> 00:01:48,413
particles are already packed closely together, leaving very

34
00:01:48,471 --> 00:01:50,190
little space between them.

35
00:01:50,805 --> 00:01:52,628
Think about an ice cube.

36
00:01:52,906 --> 00:01:56,622
It maintains its shape even when you place it on a plate

37
00:01:56,714 --> 00:02:00,662
because the water molecules are held in fixed positions within

38
00:02:00,709 --> 00:02:01,951
the solid structure.

39
00:02:02,288 --> 00:02:04,006
Now, let us consider liquids.

40
00:02:04,285 --> 00:02:08,139
In a liquid, the particles remain close together, but

41
00:02:08,174 --> 00:02:11,808
they are arranged irregularly and can move past

42
00:02:11,889 --> 00:02:12,632
one another.

43
00:02:12,911 --> 00:02:16,986
The forces of attraction between particles are still important,

44
00:02:17,264 --> 00:02:21,014
but the particles have sufficient energy to move around each other.

45
00:02:21,467 --> 00:02:25,136
This explains why liquids can flow and take the shape

46
00:02:25,182 --> 00:02:26,483
of their containers.

47
00:02:26,877 --> 00:02:30,534
For example, when you pour water into a glass, it

48
00:02:30,604 --> 00:02:33,251
takes the shape of the bottom of the glass.

49
00:02:33,646 --> 00:02:38,046
When you pour the same water into a bottle, its shape changes,

50
00:02:38,383 --> 00:02:42,005
but its volume remains approximately the same.

51
00:02:42,458 --> 00:02:46,266
Liquids have a fixed volume but no fixed shape.

52
00:02:46,661 --> 00:02:50,434
Like solids, liquids are difficult to compress because

53
00:02:50,469 --> 00:02:53,441
there is very little space between their particles.

54
00:02:53,778 --> 00:02:56,146
Now, let us examine gases.

55
00:02:56,390 --> 00:03:00,059
In a gas, particles are much farther apart than in

56
00:03:00,175 --> 00:03:01,824
solids and liquids.

57
00:03:02,102 --> 00:03:05,957
The forces of attraction between them are generally negligible,

58
00:03:06,166 --> 00:03:10,566
and the particles move rapidly and randomly in all directions.

59
00:03:10,902 --> 00:03:14,861
Because the particles move freely, a gas has neither

60
00:03:14,931 --> 00:03:17,868
a fixed shape nor a fixed volume.

61
00:03:18,205 --> 00:03:21,920
Instead, it spreads out to fill the entire container.

62
00:03:22,442 --> 00:03:26,123
For example, when perfume is sprayed in one corner of a

63
00:03:26,204 --> 00:03:30,151
room, its gaseous particles spread through the air.

64
00:03:30,732 --> 00:03:33,321
This process is called diffusion.

65
00:03:34,006 --> 00:03:37,663
Gases can also be compressed easily because there are

66
00:03:37,709 --> 00:03:40,043
large spaces between their particles.

67
00:03:40,472 --> 00:03:42,980
Consider air inside a syringe.

68
00:03:43,317 --> 00:03:47,566
When you push the plunger while blocking the opening, the air occupies

69
00:03:47,659 --> 00:03:51,397
a smaller volume because the gas particles move closer

70
00:03:51,467 --> 00:03:52,163
together.

71
00:03:52,779 --> 00:03:56,715
Now, let us compare ice, liquid water and steam.

72
00:03:57,167 --> 00:04:00,952
All three contain the same chemical substance, H two

73
00:04:01,034 --> 00:04:01,428
O.

74
00:04:01,881 --> 00:04:05,678
The difference is not the chemical identity of the particles,

75
00:04:05,887 --> 00:04:09,033
but their arrangement, movement and energy.

76
00:04:09,370 --> 00:04:13,015
In ice, the water molecules vibrate about fixed

77
00:04:13,073 --> 00:04:13,874
positions.

78
00:04:14,211 --> 00:04:17,984
In liquid water, the molecules move past one another.

79
00:04:18,251 --> 00:04:22,605
In steam, the water molecules move rapidly and randomly,

80
00:04:22,802 --> 00:04:24,939
with large spaces between them.

81
00:04:25,310 --> 00:04:29,304
Here is an important G C S E examination

82
00:04:29,374 --> 00:04:29,826
point.

83
00:04:30,059 --> 00:04:33,692
When a substance changes state, its particles do not

84
00:04:33,762 --> 00:04:35,666
become larger or smaller.

85
00:04:35,910 --> 00:04:40,043
It is their arrangement, movement and separation that change.

86
00:04:40,438 --> 00:04:42,597
Let us test your understanding.

87
00:04:42,984 --> 00:04:45,051
Imagine three containers.

88
00:04:45,376 --> 00:04:49,625
The first contains ice, the second contains liquid water,

89
00:04:49,822 --> 00:04:52,365
and the third contains water vapour.

90
00:04:52,794 --> 00:04:57,148
Which substance has particles vibrating about fixed positions?

91
00:04:57,380 --> 00:04:59,052
That is correct: ice.

92
00:04:59,435 --> 00:05:03,255
Which has particles that move past one another while remaining close

93
00:05:03,290 --> 00:05:03,917
together?

94
00:05:04,253 --> 00:05:05,345
Liquid water.

95
00:05:05,681 --> 00:05:09,478
And which has particles that are widely separated and move

96
00:05:09,524 --> 00:05:11,486
rapidly in random directions?

97
00:05:12,102 --> 00:05:13,112
Water vapour.

98
00:05:13,390 --> 00:05:17,198
Now, let us tackle a G C S E examination question.

99
00:05:17,535 --> 00:05:21,436
Explain why gases can be compressed easily, whereas

100
00:05:21,506 --> 00:05:22,783
solids cannot.

101
00:05:23,061 --> 00:05:25,639
This question is worth three marks.

102
00:05:26,034 --> 00:05:29,900
A full-mark answer would be: In a gas, particles

103
00:05:29,993 --> 00:05:33,359
are far apart, with large spaces between them.

104
00:05:33,603 --> 00:05:37,260
When pressure is applied, the particles can be pushed

105
00:05:37,353 --> 00:05:40,151
closer together, reducing the volume.

106
00:05:40,674 --> 00:05:44,435
In a solid, particles are already packed closely

107
00:05:44,493 --> 00:05:48,522
together, with very little space between them, so the volume

108
00:05:48,580 --> 00:05:50,705
cannot be reduced significantly.

109
00:05:51,320 --> 00:05:54,977
Notice that we have not simply stated that gases

110
00:05:55,047 --> 00:05:56,162
can be compressed.

111
00:05:56,487 --> 00:06:00,887
We have explained why, using the arrangement of particles.

112
00:06:01,328 --> 00:06:05,299
That is exactly what examiners are looking for when a question

113
00:06:05,415 --> 00:06:06,912
asks you to explain.

114
00:06:07,353 --> 00:06:11,254
Before we finish, remember these three key ideas.

115
00:06:11,858 --> 00:06:15,747
Solids have a fixed shape and volume because

116
00:06:15,782 --> 00:06:19,300
their particles vibrate about fixed positions.

117
00:06:19,997 --> 00:06:23,816
Liquids have a fixed volume but take the shape of their containers

118
00:06:24,060 --> 00:06:26,858
because their particles can move past one another.

119
00:06:27,380 --> 00:06:31,467
Gases have no fixed shape or volume because their particles

120
00:06:31,583 --> 00:06:34,544
move freely and are widely separated.

121
